<p><i>This transcript was generated from an .mp4 recording using Adobe Premiere’s English speech-to-text auto-transcription feature. Members of our INF STD 270 group (Clare Britton, Claire Downey, Lily Grossbard, and Emery Joseph) conducted this interview over zoom on February 17, 2026 with Mark Toscano, Senior Film Preservationist at the Academy Film Archive in Los Angeles, CA. This transcript has been edited for clarity, and certain segments have been redacted at the discretion of the group members. Minor grammatical changes are not noted; major redactions are noted with ellipses: […]
</i><br><br><b>Question</b>: We were wondering if you could just start out with your name, and your job description.<br><br><b>Answer</b>: Sure. My name is Mark Toscano. I'm a senior film preservationist at the Academy Film Archive. I have an official job description. I could dig up and read for you if you want, but essentially, my job comprises the oversight of preservation and restoration projects at the archive, photochemical and digital and both sometimes. It is a mix of the technical, philosophical, and project management aspects. And curatorial. A lot of it is dealing with people, dealing with filmmakers or their estates. But also dealing with labs, lab technicians. I'm overseeing the projects and I'm evaluating the results I'm coming up with the plans for what to do. But obviously, I'm relying on a lot of other really amazing people, at film labs especially, and audio houses. And, although it's not part of my job description technically, what I've specialized in for the nearly 23 years I've been here – I kind of can't believe it's been that long – is independent and experimental film, including animation documentaries, but also some independent features as well, that are not particularly experimental but were independently made. And actually, with the exception of a few projects, like helping make prints of a few things – I oversaw making like some Miyazaki prints at one point, and I worked on My Own Private Idaho, making prints of that – other than that, all the preservation work I've done is exclusively on independent things. So no major studio projects all this time, which I'm very proud of, actually.
